    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_8920cf6deee305bb0aa2b93eab76825826c9944a4ee784fc71b58e1c53dd78d9",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: the source defines carrier waves as emergent density fluctuations requiring many repelling zeros, then explains why finite-height zeta rigidity and frequent resetting keep such waves small. It exposes a hidden calibration choice: a rescaled U(kappa N) polynomial can preserve mean zero spacing yet has more room for density sloshing than kappa independent U(N) blocks, producing larger carriers, maxima, and moments. Interpretation: matching microscopic mean spacing does not identify the model because system size controls collective modes. Boundary: resetting and anticorrelation are explanatory interpretations, and independent blocks are not an exact construction of zeta.",
