    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_c56ded4fe1b41299c8de140869b205b7250e5f2a916bfd8b89c5ee8eb8c5f1fd",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The smoothed hybrid product makes prime and zero descriptions adjustable coordinates rather than causal rivals: increasing its cutoff shifts explanatory load toward prime powers and nearer zeros. The prime-sum and matrix-trace representations then motivate the same log-correlated rough field and clustered carrier-scale maxima, while the randomized-zeta limit factors that rough interval structure into a shared GMC distribution times a smooth model-specific correction. The closing zero-first density account is therefore a complementary scale description, not an identification of density waves with GMC.",
